{"name":"Wavy Navy","hex":"#006597","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wavy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Rising or swelling in waves.","Full of waves."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of wavey ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wavy"},{"term":"navy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A country's entire maritime military force, including ships and personnel.","A governmental department in charge of a country's maritime military force."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the dark blue colour of navy blue.","Belonging to the navy; typical of the navy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/navy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wavy Navy (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Wavy Navy is a video game designed by Rodney McAuley for the Apple II and published by Sirius Software in 1983. Versions for the Atari 8-bit computers and Commodore 64 were released the same year. Wavy Navy is a nautically themed fixed shooter with left and right controls to move the player's PT boat, but there is an additional complexity as the boat moves up and down with the large ocean waves that scroll beneath it. Some reviewers found that the waves added an interesting twist, while others called it too similar to other fixed shooters like Galaxian."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/af/Wavy_Navy_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wavy_Navy_(video_game)","wikidata":"Q25209571"},{"title":"Royal Naval Reserve","blocks":[{"text":"The Royal Naval Reserve (RNR) is one of the two volunteer reserve forces of the Royal Navy in the United Kingdom. Together with the Royal Marines Reserve, they form the Maritime Reserve. The present RNR was formed by merging the original Royal Naval Reserve, created in 1859, and the Royal Naval Volunteer Reserve (RNVR), created in 1903. The Royal Naval Reserve has seen action in World War I, World War II, the Iraq War and the War in Afghanistan."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Naval_ensign_of_the_United_Kingdom.svg/330px-Naval_ensign_of_the_United_Kingdom.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Naval_Reserve","wikidata":"Q2778989"},{"title":"Navy","blocks":[{"text":"A navy, naval force, military maritime fleet, war navy, or maritime force is the branch of a state's armed forces principally designated for naval and amphibious warfare; namely, lake-borne, riverine, littoral, or ocean-borne combat operations and related functions. It includes anything conducted by surface ships, amphibious ships, submarines, and seaborne aviation, as well as ancillary support, communications, training, and other fields."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/The_Hermione_being_escorted_by_the_USS_Mitscher_%28DDG-57%29_%281%29.jpg/330px-The_Hermione_being_escorted_by_the_USS_Mitscher_%28DDG-57%29_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navy","wikidata":"Q4508"},{"title":"Navy blue","blocks":[{"text":"Navy blue, also known simply as navy, is a dark shade of the color blue. The name navy blue originally referred to the color of uniforms worn by officers in the Royal Navy. In the late 18th century, the Royal Navy adopted the color for its sailors' uniforms, partly due to the practical reason that dark colors were less prone to showing dirt and wear during long sea voyages. The color became so associated with naval service that it came to be known simply as \"navy blue.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Flickr_-_Official_U.S._Navy_Imagery_-_The_Blue_Angels_fly_in_formation..jpg/330px-Flickr_-_Official_U.S._Navy_Imagery_-_The_Blue_Angels_fly_in_formation..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navy_blue","wikidata":"Q5975887"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1c/Wavy_Navy_%28video_game%29_Atari_8-bit_PAL_screenshot.png/330px-Wavy_Navy_%28video_game%29_Atari_8-bit_PAL_screenshot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1c/Wavy_Navy_%28video_game%29_Atari_8-bit_PAL_screenshot.png/330px-Wavy_Navy_%28video_game%29_Atari_8-bit_PAL_screenshot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The player's boat is at bottom-center, between two waves, firing upward at enemy planes and helicopters.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWavy_Navy_(video_game)_Atari_8-bit_PAL_screenshot.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Swordplay_%286388042307%29.jpg/500px-Swordplay_%286388042307%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Swordplay_%286388042307%29.jpg/500px-Swordplay_%286388042307%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Members of the Royal Naval Reserve training at Tramore, County Waterford, c. 1905","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASwordplay_(6388042307).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Lieutenant_J_S_Nash_%288358483892%29.jpg/500px-Lieutenant_J_S_Nash_%288358483892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Lieutenant_J_S_Nash_%288358483892%29.jpg/500px-Lieutenant_J_S_Nash_%288358483892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A First World War RNR Lieutenant wearing the \"wavy navy\" rank insignia of reserve officers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.